Z||000004||fierospeeder||10-28-2001||02:47 AM||||i cant remember what the "chemisty term/physics term is"  eventhough i love science and we made babies.<P><BR>I seen it on tv.  Sometimes when you boil the water, it will appear that it hasn't really boiled.   Once you disturb the water with an object, it will cause a reaction and the water will shoot up in the air hitting your face and burning you.<P>I cant remember what causes the molecules to act this way, unless someone can explain further.||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||10-28-2001||03:40 AM||||I guess it is known as nucleation. The heated liquid has to have a rough area to act upon before physical boiling can be initiated. However, this has been classified as an "urban Legend":  <A HREF="http://www.nerdherd.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nerdherd.com/</A> <BR>click on 'chain letters'<BR>scroll down to 'science/nature/disease<BR>click on 'hazards of boiling water in microwaves'<BR>or go to: <A HREF="http://www.urbanlegends.com/ulz/microwave.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.urbanlegends.com/ulz/microwave.html</A> <BR>Evidently, it can occur, but extremely unlikely.<P>science is like sex-sometimes good things comes from it, but that is not why we do it. <P><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||10-28-2001||09:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>You have a Whirlpool? Figured you'd have a 'Radar-Range' from Amanna.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would be RaYdar Range. <BR>And I didn't even think about it. You beat be to it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>My wife, the chemistry/physics lady, told me that the "boiling water in the microwave" thing is absolutely true. Under the right conditions it can boil up in your face. Seems like it had to do with different layers of the water being heated unevenly. When you move the cup, you disturb the layers.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierospeeder||10-28-2001||03:06 PM||||ya that was it, there are no rough surfaces for the water to cause it to go into a boil.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||05:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>I guess it is known as nucleation. The heated liquid has to have a rough area to act upon before physical boiling can be initiated. However, this has been classified as an "urban Legend"</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, it isn't. I can recreate this experiment 9 out of 10 times in my microwave and is the reason I don't boil water in my microwave anymore. The water will indeed not boil unless there's "movement". When water is boiled on a stove, the water is heated from the bottom causing a constant water flow since the colder water on top is heavier than the warmer water at the bottom. In a microwave, all water is heated equally and can stay in liquid form while temperature rises <B>above</B> boiling point. Since no gas is formed, the water can reach temperatures well over boiling point. The only reason why boiling water stays at a constant temperature is because evaporation needs a lot of energy which is taken from the liquid which then cools down.<P>Using the same priniciple, it's possible to cool water to temperatures way below freezing point while still in liquid state. In fact, this is quite easy to reproduce. When it freezes outside, take a cup of water and place it somewhere outside where it isn't disturbed by wind (of course, you can forget this experiment if you live near a road). The next morning, chances are the water is still liquid. Tap the cup lightly and you'll see the water freeze over instantly (very neat).||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||10-28-2001||07:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff:<P><B>Using the same priniciple, it's possible to cool water to temperatures way below freezing point while still in liquid state. In fact, this is quite easy to reproduce. When it freezes outside, take a cup of water and place it somewhere outside where it isn't disturbed by wind (of course, you can forget this experiment if you live near a road). The next morning, chances are the water is still liquid. Tap the cup lightly and you'll see the water freeze over instantly (very neat). </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The chemisty/physics lady is scratching her head.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||10-28-2001||07:35 PM||||That sounds pretty neat. Can you duplicate this phenomenom in a freezer?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||07:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>The chemisty/physics lady is scratching her head.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We used to do that all the time back in high-school. Very popular experiment in the winter.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>And yes, you can duplicate this experiment in a freezer. If you've got one of those "quiet" freezers. Haven't you ever put a bottle in the freezer to cool it, forgot about it and the moment you take the bottle out of the freezer you see it instantly freeze?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||10-28-2001||08:00 PM||||The water freezing thing gets reproduced everyday in my classroom! I keep several bottles of water in my mini-fridge at school. When you get the water out, it is completly liquid. I let one of the kids shake the bottle for me and they get a kick out of watching it turn to ice. Even neater is to thump the bottle and watch the ice form from the spot of impact.||||reg||1
